Z||000059||Cliff Pennock||11-08-2001||06:06 PM||||Just to put in my 2c:<P>I agree that the guy went a little overboard. Sure the kids did something wrong and sure they needed to get punished but what he did was wrong. If he beated the kid up physically, I'm pretty sure everybody would agree that he went to far. Well, he beat up the kid up alright - mentally. The kid will for sure remember this day for the rest of his life. And when he is older he'll realize what this guy actually did and he will try to get even. He will associate richness with this gut-disgusting feeling he has. Here's your typical "he was always such a nice guy, I have no idea why he shot innocent bystanders from the top of a building" in the making.<P>A thief is a thief, but a kid is a kid. At this age, you are leaving impressions that will never go away. And the impression he will have from this incident is that he was punished waaaaaay too severe for the crime committed. Trust me. The guy did not do a good thing. Ask any psychologist.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000060||stsmithgt||11-08-2001||06:19 PM||||That kid could develope a hatred towards rich people and end up choosing to live homeless on the streets like so many others do.  It doesn't matter if his parents have money and treated him like a normal kid.  OR! he can use those cells in his head that form what's called a brain and figure out that he did something wrong without this event even effecting him in the future other than reminding him to avoid cituations like that. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  That pic above is a joke(good or bad) if all didn't get it. Police will handle the cituation, it's their job.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000061||DRH||11-08-2001||06:59 PM||||Good summary Cliff.<P>Come on guys. The kid is 8, maybe 10, definitely not a teen. Nobody here did something they shouldn't have at that age? Lifted a candy bar? took a dollar outta mom's purse? nothing??? Not even on a DARE???<P>The kid was wrong... but not THAT wrong.<P>To top it all off it sounds like he got the wrong kid. The OTHER kid obviously had the valve caps. The OTHER kid knew enough to get away. The OTHER kid's parents wouldn't cooperate. Anybody want to guess which kid had the idea to begin with???<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000062||BN Boomer||11-08-2001||11:00 PM||||Geez, some of you guys honestly make me want to puke on this one. This kid needed to be made aware that there are consequences for one's actions and they can be quite severe. I don't see much difference between this and the time I got 2 whacks on the a** with a cricket board paddle from the vice principal for shooting rubber bands at Mrs. Mason, my 7th grade math teacher. I can tell you this much, I never shot another rubber band at a teacher. I doubt that this kid will steal another valve stem cover again. Lesson learned, no harm done. I doubt that this kid if emotionally scarred for life. What a crock. <P>He should be glad all he got was a severe ass chewing instead of getting caught doing this in a country where he may have well lost his hand for being a thief. I wonder if Michael Fey still goes around vandalizing cars.||||reg||1
Z||000063||DRH||11-08-2001||11:43 PM||||Boomer,<P>No argument the kid needed disciplined. I just don't think it was that a**hole's right to do it, nor do I think he did it in the right manner. Discipline should be administered by someone who has the child's best interest in mind as I'm sure your vice principal did. It shouldn't be done by some a**hole who couldn't care less about the kid and is doing it in retaliation.<P>What was the lesson the kid learned from: "if you dont get me back my valve stems im taking your bike."? If your bigger than someone and you don't like what they did it's OK to steal from them???<P>THEN he brags about making a "little" profit off the parents. Real upstanding guy there...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <P><BR>||||reg||1
